  m_text: "<u>Down: </u>\n1. Malicious or uncontrolled AI systems that behave in
    harmful ways against their creators, users, or society.\n3. A scam where attackers
    offer a service or benefit in exchange for sensitive information.. \n4. An act
    of secretly monitoring an organization to gather sensitive information.\n5. A
    program that secretly records what you type.\n6. An attack that guesses passwords
    repeatedly. \n8. A scam where attackers use fake relationships or attraction
    to gain trust and extract information.  \n9. A software vulnerability unknown
    to its owners, developers or anyone capable of mitigating it. \n12. Malware that
    hides itself to avoid detection.\u202F\n13. Professionals who simulate real-world
    attacks to test an organization\u2019s security defences.\n15. A cyber-attack
    where a fake login or browser window appears inside a real website to trick users.
    \n\n<u>Across: </u>\n2. A secure Environment used to safely test suspicious files.
    \n7. A hidden part of the internet not shown on search engines. \n10. An attack
    where hackers inject malicious code into a legitimate web page that is frequented
    by their targets. \n11. An Abbreviation where cybercriminals impersonate business
    executives via email. \n14. An attack that overwhelms a system with traffic.
    \n16. A scam where attackers create a false story or identity to trick people
    into sharing information. \n17. A cyber-attack where someone secretly intercepts
    communication between two parties.\n18. A cyber-attack where an attacker sits
    between a user and a service to intercept and manipulate communication, often
    during login.\n \n"
